1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Pick up an iPhone tonight,

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,408
and you are not holding a telephone.

3
00:00:03,408 --> 00:00:08,616
You are holding the most profitable consumer object ever engineered by the human species.

4
00:00:08,616 --> 00:00:13,080
A seamless slab of titanium, sapphire glass, and microscopic silicon

5
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,840
that generates more net profit in a single ninety-day quarter

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:19,704
than Nike, Coca-Cola, and McDonald’s combined.

7
00:00:19,704 --> 00:00:24,312
Behind that glass sits a corporate empire valued at over three trillion dollars.

8
00:00:24,312 --> 00:00:29,928
That is larger than the entire gross domestic product of the United Kingdom, France, or India.

9
00:00:29,928 --> 00:00:34,508
Its cash reserves could purchase entire commercial airline fleets

10
00:00:34,508 --> 00:00:41,160
or defense contractors in cash before the markets close for lunch.

11
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:46,368
Over one billion human beings carry this device in their pockets every second of the day.

12
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:48,288
When an alarm rings in Sydney,

13
00:00:48,288 --> 00:00:50,188
a podcast plays in Tokyo,

14
00:00:50,188 --> 00:00:52,408
or a contactless card taps in London,

15
00:00:52,408 --> 00:00:53,472
Apple quietly takes its toll.

16
00:00:53,472 --> 00:00:57,600
It has become the default sensory apparatus for modern human civilization.

17
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:02,616
Yet the entire empire is constructed on an illusion.

18
00:01:02,616 --> 00:01:05,056
Smooth rounded corners.

19
00:01:05,056 --> 00:01:06,676
Minimalist white boxes that slide open with a slow pneumatic glide.

20
00:01:06,676 --> 00:01:08,356
Silk-wrapped charging cords.

21
00:01:08,356 --> 00:01:09,912
Friendly, lowercase typography.

22
00:01:09,912 --> 00:01:13,592
Apple does not sell consumer electronics.

23
00:01:13,592 --> 00:01:14,856
Apple sells an identity.

24
00:01:14,856 --> 00:01:17,156
It sells the flattering, comforting belief that by tapping on their glass, you are not merely a corporate worker checking emails at midnight.

25
00:01:17,156 --> 00:01:18,776
You are an artist.

26
00:01:18,776 --> 00:01:21,736
A creator.

27
00:01:21,736 --> 00:01:23,760
A rebel who thinks a little differently than the uninspired masses around you.

28
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:26,784
It is the greatest psychological trick in corporate history.

29
00:01:26,784 --> 00:01:31,440
Because fifty years ago, there was no glass spaceship campus rising out of Cupertino apricot orchards.

30
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,400
There were no pristine marble retail temples.

31
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,648
There were no billion-dollar marketing campaigns.

32
00:01:36,648 --> 00:01:39,368
There was only a drafty suburban garage in Los Altos, California,

33
00:01:39,368 --> 00:01:43,224
with oil stains soaked into the concrete floor,

34
00:01:43,224 --> 00:01:44,804
two college dropouts who had barely twenty-five dollars between them,

35
00:01:44,804 --> 00:01:46,752
and an act of petty corporate theft that almost destroyed their friendship before it even began.

36
00:01:46,752 --> 00:01:49,412
To understand Apple, you have to understand that it was never an engineering company.

37
00:01:49,412 --> 00:01:51,840
It was an uneasy partnership between an engineering saint and a ruthless commercial prophet.

38
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:52,800
In 1971, Steve Wozniak and Steve Jobs were teenage misfits in the smoggy suburbs of Silicon Valley.

39
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:55,020
Wozniak was a gentle, childlike prodigy who built electronic circuits for the pure, uncorrupted joy of making electrons dance.

40
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:56,420
Jobs was a barefoot, brooding wanderer who attended Reed College, dropped out after one semester,

41
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:57,640
slept on dorm room floors, and returned deposit bottles for five cents to buy food.

42
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,088
Their first commercial venture was illegal.

43
00:01:59,088 --> 00:01:59,488
Wozniak discovered that toy whistles packed inside Cap'n Crunch cereal boxes emitted a tone at exactly twenty-six hundred hertz.

44
00:01:59,488 --> 00:01:59,888
That precise frequency fooled AT&T’s automated telephone switches into granting free, untraceable long-distance calls across the globe.

45
00:01:59,888 --> 00:02:00,668
Woz built an electronic version called a Blue Box.

46
00:02:00,668 --> 00:02:01,068
Jobs realized they shouldn't just prank phone companies—they should sell the boxes to college students for one hundred and fifty dollars each.

47
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:01,468
They used one to call the Vatican in Rome, with Wozniak pretending to be Henry Kissinger asking to speak to the Pope in the middle of the night.

48
00:02:01,468 --> 00:02:02,188
Then came the incident that revealed the cold shadow inside Steve Jobs.

49
00:02:02,188 --> 00:02:02,588
In 1975, Atari founder Nolan Bushnell hired Jobs to build a single-player version of Pong called Breakout.

50
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:02,988
Bushnell offered a bonus: seven hundred dollars for the game,

51
00:02:02,988 --> 00:02:03,388
plus an extra one hundred dollars for every computer chip eliminated from the design.

52
00:02:03,388 --> 00:02:03,788
Jobs knew he lacked the engineering chops to do it,

53
00:02:03,788 --> 00:02:04,188
so he begged Wozniak to help, promising they would split the payout fifty-fifty.

54
00:02:04,188 --> 00:02:04,588
Wozniak worked for four straight nights without sleep, contracting mono,

55
00:02:04,588 --> 00:02:04,988
and designed a masterpiece that cut fifty chips from the circuit board.

56
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:05,388
Atari paid Jobs a massive five-thousand-dollar bonus.

57
00:02:05,388 --> 00:02:05,788
Jobs returned to the garage, looked his exhausted best friend in the eye, and told him Atari had paid only seven hundred dollars.

58
00:02:05,788 --> 00:02:19,440
He handed Wozniak three hundred and fifty dollars,

59
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,760
and pocketed the remaining four thousand six hundred and fifty dollars for himself.

60
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,880
Years later, when Wozniak discovered the truth, he broke down and cried.

61
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:37,824
But he didn't walk away.

62
00:02:37,824 --> 00:02:39,104
Because despite Jobs’s ruthless deceit, Woz knew that without Jobs, his brilliant inventions would sit in a cardboard box on a bedroom floor forever.

63
00:02:39,104 --> 00:02:41,472
In early 1976, Wozniak had an epiphany that changed computing history.

64
00:02:41,472 --> 00:02:48,456
At the time, computers were terrifying, multi-million-dollar metal cabinets locked behind plate glass in air-conditioned basements of banks and military installations.

65
00:02:48,456 --> 00:02:58,800
They were fed paper punch cards by men in white lab coats.

66
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:04,104
The idea that an ordinary human would want a computer in their living room was considered absurd.

67
00:03:04,104 --> 00:03:36,792
Ken Olsen, founder of Digital Equipment Corporation, famously mocked: "There is no reason anyone would want a computer in their home."

68
00:03:36,792 --> 00:03:57,048
Wozniak disagreed.

69
00:03:57,048 --> 00:04:07,488
Sitting on the floor of his cramped apartment, he wired an ordinary keyboard directly to a microprocessor,

70
00:04:07,488 --> 00:04:09,148
and hooked the output to a portable black-and-white television screen.

71
00:04:09,148 --> 00:04:12,428
He pressed a key.

72
00:04:12,428 --> 00:04:14,352
And for the first time in human history, the letter appeared instantly on the screen in front of him.

73
00:04:14,352 --> 00:04:16,212
No punch cards.

74
00:04:16,212 --> 00:04:17,772
No paper printouts.

75
00:04:17,772 --> 00:04:20,640
No waiting overnight.

76
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:23,328
It was instantaneous, personal computing.

77
00:04:23,328 --> 00:04:26,808
Woz was so proud that he took his schematics to the Homebrew Computer Club in Menlo Park

78
00:04:26,808 --> 00:05:29,160
and gave them away to fellow hobbyists for free.

79
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:42,360
When Jobs saw the machine, he intervened immediately.

80
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:46,060
"Stop giving it away," Jobs demanded.

81
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:57,336
"Let's build circuit boards and sell them."

82
00:05:57,336 --> 00:05:59,656
To raise the seed capital, Jobs sold his beloved red-and-white Volkswagen microbus for a few hundred dollars.

83
00:05:59,656 --> 00:06:07,848
Wozniak sold his prized Hewlett-Packard scientific calculator for five hundred.

84
00:06:07,848 --> 00:06:11,268
Combined, they had thirteen hundred dollars.

85
00:06:11,268 --> 00:06:14,304
They incorporated on April Fools’ Day, 1976.

86
00:06:14,304 --> 00:06:23,376
Jobs named it Apple Computer after spending time pruning Gravenstein trees on an Oregon orchard commune.

87
00:06:23,376 --> 00:06:26,276
It sounded friendly, non-threatening,

88
00:06:26,276 --> 00:06:49,896
and crucially, it appeared before Atari in the phone book.

89
00:06:49,896 --> 00:06:54,432
Jobs marched into the Byte Shop in Mountain View and pitched their naked circuit board to owner Paul Terrell.

90
00:06:54,432 --> 00:06:58,608
Terrell looked at the board of exposed wires and made an unexpected counteroffer:

91
00:06:58,608 --> 00:06:59,748
he would buy fifty units, but only if they were fully assembled, working computers.

92
00:06:59,748 --> 00:07:01,368
Price: five hundred dollars each, cash on delivery.

93
00:07:01,368 --> 00:07:04,152
Twenty-five thousand dollars.

94
00:07:04,152 --> 00:07:10,680
It was more money than either of them had ever contemplated.

95
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,768
The garage on Crist Drive became an assembly plant.

96
00:07:12,768 --> 00:07:14,868
Jobs’s sister was paid one dollar per board to solder chip sockets.

97
00:07:14,868 --> 00:07:16,872
Jobs badgered electronic suppliers into giving him thirty days of credit on parts.

98
00:07:16,872 --> 00:07:19,452
They delivered all fifty computers on time in wooden boxes.

99
00:07:19,452 --> 00:07:30,816
The Apple I was an underground hit among hardcore hobbyists.

100
00:07:30,816 --> 00:07:37,200
But Jobs was already dissatisfied.

101
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:43,368
It was still just a raw circuit board for nerds with soldering irons.

102
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:48,744
Jobs understood that a true revolution wouldn't happen until computers became consumer appliances.

103
00:07:48,744 --> 00:07:54,360
Friendly, beautiful, quiet machines that worked straight out of the box, as simple as a kitchen toaster.

104
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:58,296
Woz sat down and engineered his magnum opus: the Apple II.

105
00:07:58,296 --> 00:08:05,712
It featured built-in color graphics, sound, game ports, and expansion slots.

106
00:08:05,712 --> 00:08:09,552
Jobs obsessed over the industrial enclosure.

107
00:08:09,552 --> 00:08:09,952
No visible metal screws.

108
00:08:09,952 --> 00:08:11,252
No ugly, noisy cooling fan—the machine had to be completely silent,

109
00:08:11,252 --> 00:08:12,288
requiring a custom lightweight switching power supply.

110
00:08:12,288 --> 00:08:16,392
He spent weeks examining forty different plastic color swatches to find the precise, warm shade of beige.

111
00:08:16,392 --> 00:08:21,600
To manufacture it, they needed serious capital.

112
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:23,620
Enter Mike Markkula, an Intel millionaire who recognized genius in the mismatched duo.

113
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:25,224
Markkula invested ninety-two thousand dollars of his own money,

114
00:08:25,224 --> 00:08:29,084
secured a quarter-million-dollar line of credit,

115
00:08:29,084 --> 00:08:29,484
and instituted Apple's corporate philosophy: empathy with the customer, focus,

116
00:08:29,484 --> 00:08:30,888
and "imputing"—the belief that people judge a book by its cover, and that beautiful design signals quality before the power switch is even flipped.

117
00:08:30,888 --> 00:08:36,864
The Apple II debuted at the 1977 West Coast Computer Faire.

118
00:08:36,864 --> 00:08:41,144
While competitors displayed drab grey metal industrial boxes,

119
00:08:41,144 --> 00:08:45,744
Apple’s booth glowed with backlit acrylic signs and friendly beige appliances.

120
00:08:45,744 --> 00:08:52,968
Then, in 1979, two Harvard students wrote VisiCalc—the world’s first electronic spreadsheet.

121
00:08:52,968 --> 00:08:57,432
Suddenly, corporate executives weren't buying an Apple II to play breakout games.

122
00:08:57,432 --> 00:09:02,880
They were buying it because it calculated corporate financial forecasts in twenty seconds instead of twenty days.

123
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:06,140
Apple became an economic rocket ship.

124
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:11,976
Revenue skyrocketed from eight million dollars in 1978 to one hundred and seventeen million in 1980.

125
00:09:11,976 --> 00:09:16,920
On December 12, 1980, Apple Computer went public on Wall Street.

126
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:21,648
It was the largest stock market debut since Ford Motor Company in 1956.

127
00:09:21,648 --> 00:09:26,112
In an afternoon, three hundred Apple employees became instant millionaires.

128
00:09:26,112 --> 00:09:35,184
Steve Jobs, at age twenty-five, was suddenly worth two hundred and fifty-six million dollars.

129
00:09:35,184 --> 00:09:39,048
He had conquered Silicon Valley before his hair had even begun to thin.

130
00:09:39,048 --> 00:09:39,808
And that is when the hubris took control.

131
00:09:39,808 --> 00:09:40,388
Wealth amplified Jobs's worst instincts.

132
00:09:40,388 --> 00:09:58,512
He became convinced of his absolute infallibility.

133
00:09:58,512 --> 00:10:11,352
In December 1979, Jobs visited the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.

134
00:10:11,352 --> 00:10:17,760
In exchange for one million dollars of pre-IPO Apple stock, Xerox scientists agreed to give Jobs a tour of their secret computer laboratories.

135
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:18,680
They showed him three revolutionary technologies:

136
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:21,120
object-oriented programming,

137
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:24,020
networked Ethernet,

138
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,776
and a graphical user interface where a handheld mouse moved a cursor across virtual windows on a screen.

139
00:10:25,776 --> 00:10:29,116
Jobs stared at the screen, his pupils dilated in pure shock.

140
00:10:29,116 --> 00:10:42,792
Xerox was a copier company whose corporate executives had zero idea what their scientists had built.

141
00:10:42,792 --> 00:10:43,852
Jobs began pacing the room, shouting: "Why aren't you doing anything with this?

142
00:10:43,852 --> 00:10:45,672
This is the greatest thing!

143
00:10:45,672 --> 00:10:47,112
You are sitting on a gold mine!"

144
00:10:47,112 --> 00:10:48,456
Jobs raced back to Apple, determined to build the graphical interface into Apple’s next corporate machine: the Lisa.

145
00:10:48,456 --> 00:10:52,256
It was a disaster.

146
00:10:52,256 --> 00:11:22,152
Jobs meddled with engineering teams, changed architectural specs daily,

147
00:11:22,152 --> 00:11:41,712
and drove costs into the stratosphere.

148
00:11:41,712 --> 00:11:58,416
When the Lisa finally launched in 1983, it carried a price tag of nine thousand nine hundred and ninety-five dollars—nearly thirty thousand dollars in today’s money.

149
00:11:58,416 --> 00:12:00,916
It flopped catastrophically.

150
00:12:00,916 --> 00:12:03,216
Apple eventually hauled thousands of unsold Lisas into the Utah desert

151
00:12:03,216 --> 00:12:04,992
and buried them in a landfill.

152
00:12:04,992 --> 00:12:09,096
Humiliated, the board stripped Jobs of all authority on the Lisa project.

153
00:12:09,096 --> 00:12:11,816
Furious, Jobs sought revenge.

154
00:12:11,816 --> 00:12:14,904
He marched across the street into an annex building and hijacked a small, experimental skunkworks project led by Jef Raskin: the Macintosh.

155
00:12:14,904 --> 00:12:16,992
Jobs declared open civil war against the rest of Apple.

156
00:12:16,992 --> 00:12:20,856
He hoisted a black pirate flag with a skull and rainbow Apple eye patch above the Macintosh building

157
00:12:20,856 --> 00:12:23,640
and told his team: "It is better to be a pirate than to join the Navy."

158
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:31,128
The Macintosh engineers worked eighty to ninety hours a week,

159
00:12:31,128 --> 00:12:32,748
wearing t-shirts that boasted of their exhaustion.

160
00:12:32,748 --> 00:12:34,828
Jobs pushed them through what colleagues called his "Reality Distortion Field"—

161
00:12:34,828 --> 00:12:36,488
a hypnotic blend of charismatic charm, relentless will, and psychological abuse.

162
00:12:36,488 --> 00:12:38,112
If an engineer presented code Jobs didn't like, he would scream that it was garbage.

163
00:12:38,112 --> 00:12:42,360
If an engineer solved an impossible problem, Jobs would return two days later and claim the idea as his own.

164
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:45,220
Yet they worshipped him.

165
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:47,616
Because he forced them to achieve engineering feats they did not believe were possible.

166
00:12:47,616 --> 00:12:48,576
On February 10, 1982, Jobs brought the entire team together with a bottle of champagne,

167
00:12:48,576 --> 00:12:49,896
laid out a large sheet of drafting paper, and had all forty engineers sign their names.

168
00:12:49,896 --> 00:12:50,956
He then had their signatures engraved permanently inside the plastic tooling of every single Macintosh case.

169
00:12:50,956 --> 00:12:51,876
"Real artists sign their work," Jobs proclaimed.

170
00:12:51,876 --> 00:12:52,824
To launch the computer, Jobs demanded an advertising earthquake.

171
00:12:52,824 --> 00:12:56,352
He hired Hollywood director Ridley Scott

172
00:12:56,352 --> 00:13:00,456
and spent an unprecedented seven hundred and fifty thousand dollars to create the iconic "1984" Super Bowl commercial.

173
00:13:00,456 --> 00:13:07,152
It depicted a grey, totalitarian dystopia where brainwashed masses stare at a giant video wall of Big Brother—a thinly veiled stand-in for IBM.

174
00:13:07,152 --> 00:13:13,440
Suddenly, a lone female athlete in bright red shorts sprints down the aisle

175
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:29,280
and hurls a sledgehammer through the screen, exploding it in a shower of sparks and blinding light.

176
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:32,420
Apple’s board of directors watched a screening in horror.

177
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:35,376
They thought it was reckless, arrogant, and humiliating.

178
00:13:35,376 --> 00:13:37,736
They ordered the advertising agency to sell off the Super Bowl commercial slots.

179
00:13:37,736 --> 00:13:40,856
Jobs refused.

180
00:13:40,856 --> 00:13:43,872
Steve Wozniak offered to pay for half the broadcast out of his own pocket.

181
00:13:43,872 --> 00:13:50,976
On Super Bowl Sunday, January 22, 1984, the commercial aired to ninety-six million viewers.

182
00:13:50,976 --> 00:13:56,400
It aired only once on national television, but it dominated every evening news broadcast in America.

183
00:13:56,400 --> 00:14:01,248
Two days later, Jobs walked onto the stage at the Flint Center auditorium in Cupertino.

184
00:14:01,248 --> 00:14:04,704
He wore a polka-dot bow tie.

185
00:14:04,704 --> 00:14:08,640
He pulled a small, beige rectangular computer from a canvas carrying bag,

186
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:12,840
slid a floppy disk into the drive, and stepped back.

187
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:13,240
The computer drew cursive letters on the screen: "hello".

188
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:15,596
Then, it spoke in a synthetic voice: "Hello, I am Macintosh.

189
00:14:15,596 --> 00:14:18,256
It sure is great to get out of that bag...

190
00:14:18,256 --> 00:14:19,584
Never trust a computer you cannot lift."

191
00:14:19,584 --> 00:14:21,664
The auditorium exploded into a deafening standing ovation.

192
00:14:21,664 --> 00:14:25,272
People in the front rows were weeping.

193
00:14:25,272 --> 00:14:37,440
The mouse, the desktop, the trash can, and proportional typography were officially unleashed upon the world.

194
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:39,096
Then, the cold reality hit.

195
00:14:39,096 --> 00:14:43,032
The Macintosh was visually breathtaking, but technologically crippled.

196
00:14:43,032 --> 00:14:46,432
In his obsession with design, Jobs had refused to install a cooling fan, causing the machine to overheat and warp circuit boards.

197
00:14:46,432 --> 00:14:48,672
He had limited the memory to a minuscule 128 kilobytes.

198
00:14:48,672 --> 00:14:50,856
It had no internal hard drive.

199
00:14:50,856 --> 00:14:56,544
Users had to perform the "floppy disk shuffle," ejecting and re-inserting disks dozens of times just to copy a single file.

200
00:14:56,544 --> 00:15:00,464
By late 1984, sales hit a brick wall.

201
00:15:00,464 --> 00:15:02,264
Apple was manufacturing ten thousand Macs a month and selling fewer than two thousand.

202
00:15:02,264 --> 00:15:04,920
Its highly automated manufacturing facility in Fremont, California, was running at ten percent capacity.

203
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:07,008
The company was hemorrhaging tens of millions of dollars.

204
00:15:07,008 --> 00:15:12,432
Tensions between Jobs and CEO John Sculley erupted into toxic hostility.

205
00:15:12,432 --> 00:15:17,448
Jobs had lured Sculley from Pepsi in 1983 with his famous, stinging challenge:

206
00:15:17,448 --> 00:15:22,824
"Do you want to sell sugar water for the rest of your life, or do you want to come with me and change the world?"

207
00:15:22,824 --> 00:15:29,232
Now, Sculley found himself managing an erratic, vindictive founder whose division was threatening to bankrupt the entire corporation.

208
00:15:29,232 --> 00:15:36,360
In May 1985, while Sculley was scheduled to be in China on a trade mission, Jobs plotted a palace coup to remove him and seize control of Apple.

209
00:15:36,360 --> 00:15:39,800
An executive warned Sculley.

210
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:42,936
Sculley cancelled the flight, walked into the boardroom, and forced an emergency showdown.

211
00:15:42,936 --> 00:15:48,624
He polled the board of directors one by one: "Do you back Steve, or do you back me?"

212
00:15:48,624 --> 00:15:51,584
Every single board member voted for Sculley.

213
00:15:51,584 --> 00:15:54,240
Steve Jobs was stripped of all operational responsibility.

214
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:55,820
He was banished to an empty office in an auxiliary building that employees mockingly nicknamed "Siberia."

215
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:57,984
His phone stopped ringing. Nobody came to his office.

216
00:15:57,984 --> 00:16:03,672
On September 17, 1985, Steve Jobs resigned from Apple.

217
00:16:03,672 --> 00:16:08,392
In a fit of bitter fury, he sold every single share of his Apple stock—millions of shares worth over one hundred million dollars—

218
00:16:08,392 --> 00:16:12,096
retaining only one single share so he would still receive the annual shareholder reports in the mail.

219
00:16:12,096 --> 00:16:14,116
He was thirty years old.

220
00:16:14,116 --> 00:16:15,736
He was a multi-millionaire.

221
00:16:15,736 --> 00:16:16,136
And in the eyes of Silicon Valley, he was completely finished.

222
00:16:16,136 --> 00:16:17,064
What followed was a brutal eleven-year wilderness.

223
00:16:17,064 --> 00:16:28,776
Jobs retreated to his wood-paneled mansion in Los Gatos, sat on the floor,

224
00:16:28,776 --> 00:16:54,000
and contemplated leaving tech entirely to study architecture in Europe.

225
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,180
But his pride would not let him stay down.

226
00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:57,380
He took seven million dollars of his own money

227
00:16:57,380 --> 00:16:58,840
and founded NeXT Computer, determined to build the ultimate workstation for universities.

228
00:16:58,840 --> 00:16:59,940
He paid seventy-one-year-old graphic design legend Paul Rand one hundred thousand dollars

229
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:00,900
to create the NeXT logo—a tilted black cube.

230
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:02,184
Jobs spent years and millions perfecting the machine's magnesium chassis,

231
00:17:02,184 --> 00:17:07,512
demanding perfectly precise ninety-degree internal angles that no customer would ever see.

232
00:17:07,512 --> 00:17:18,264
When the NeXT cube launched in 1988, it was an engineering marvel

233
00:17:18,264 --> 00:17:24,048
running an advanced object-oriented operating system called NeXTSTEP.

234
00:17:24,048 --> 00:17:25,348
In Switzerland, computer scientist Tim Berners-Lee used a NeXT computer to invent the World Wide Web.

235
00:17:25,348 --> 00:17:26,248
But at ten thousand dollars, universities couldn't afford it.

236
00:17:26,248 --> 00:17:28,308
NeXT sold barely fifty thousand machines over its entire lifespan.

237
00:17:28,308 --> 00:17:42,168
It was a commercial bloodbath.

238
00:17:42,168 --> 00:17:42,828
Yet Jobs had made a second bet that saved him.

239
00:17:42,828 --> 00:17:43,228
In 1986, he bought a struggling digital animation division from Star Wars director George Lucas for five million dollars.

240
00:17:43,228 --> 00:17:43,628
He named it Pixar.

241
00:17:43,628 --> 00:17:44,028
For nearly a decade, Pixar hemorrhaged cash.

242
00:17:44,028 --> 00:17:44,848
Jobs personally bankrolled the company, writing check after check to keep the payroll alive, burning over fifty million dollars of his personal fortune.

243
00:17:44,848 --> 00:17:45,248
Traditional hardware executives urged him to shut it down.

244
00:17:45,248 --> 00:17:45,648
But animators like John Lasseter were using code to make audiences feel real emotion—

245
00:17:45,648 --> 00:17:46,588
first with a jumping desk lamp named Luxo Jr.,

246
00:17:46,588 --> 00:17:46,988
and then, in November 1995, with the world's first computer-animated feature film: Toy Story.

247
00:17:46,988 --> 00:17:47,388
Toy Story became a monumental global blockbuster.

248
00:17:47,388 --> 00:17:47,788
A week later, Jobs took Pixar public on Wall Street.

249
00:17:47,788 --> 00:17:48,188
His eighty percent stake suddenly made him a billionaire in his own right, worth over one point two billion dollars.

250
00:17:48,188 --> 00:17:48,588
For the first time, Steve Jobs was independent of Apple.

251
00:17:48,588 --> 00:17:48,988
He had matured.

252
00:17:48,988 --> 00:17:59,232
He had learned patience, corporate governance,

253
00:17:59,232 --> 00:18:00,812
and how to trust creative geniuses without suffocating them.

254
00:18:00,812 --> 00:18:02,232
Meanwhile, back in Cupertino, Apple was dying a slow, humiliating death.

255
00:18:02,232 --> 00:18:02,892
Under a succession of corporate caretakers—John Sculley, Michael Spindler, and Gil Amelio—

256
00:18:02,892 --> 00:18:17,856
Apple had become a bureaucratic monstrosity.

257
00:18:17,856 --> 00:18:26,424
To appease retailers, they fractured the product line into dozens of confusing, overlapping models: Quadra, Centris, Performa, PowerBook.

258
00:18:26,424 --> 00:18:45,576
Salespeople couldn't explain the differences.

259
00:18:45,576 --> 00:18:47,916
In August 1995, Bill Gates launched Windows 95.

260
00:18:47,916 --> 00:18:50,112
Accompanied by the Rolling Stones' "Start Me Up,"

261
00:18:50,112 --> 00:18:59,808
Windows 95 copied the Mac's desktop interface, taskbar, and windows.

262
00:18:59,808 --> 00:19:02,368
It wasn't as elegant as Apple's software, but it ran on cheap personal computers built by Compaq, Dell, and HP.

263
00:19:02,368 --> 00:19:05,352
Microsoft swallowed ninety percent of the global computer market.

264
00:19:05,352 --> 00:19:09,480
Apple’s market share collapsed from sixteen percent down to an irrelevant four percent.

265
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:15,696
In 1996 alone, Apple lost over one billion dollars.

266
00:19:15,696 --> 00:19:22,104
Its aging operating system was obsolete and prone to catastrophic crashes.

267
00:19:22,104 --> 00:19:27,768
Six years and five hundred million dollars spent developing a replacement operating system code-named Copland had resulted in total failure.

268
00:19:27,768 --> 00:19:30,408
By late 1996, Apple was ninety days away from formal bankruptcy.

269
00:19:30,408 --> 00:19:32,352
Industry analysts were already drafting the company’s obituary.

270
00:19:32,352 --> 00:19:38,448
When PC maker Michael Dell was asked what Apple should do, he offered the ultimate corporate epitaph:

271
00:19:38,448 --> 00:20:44,712
"What would I do? I'd shut it down and give the money back to the shareholders."

272
00:20:44,712 --> 00:20:48,492
Desperate for an operating system, CEO Gil Amelio made a fateful inquiry.

273
00:20:48,492 --> 00:20:52,608
He looked at buying BeOS, an operating system built by former Apple executive Jean-Louis Gassée.

274
00:20:52,608 --> 00:20:53,468
But Gassée overplayed his hand, demanding three hundred million dollars.

275
00:20:53,468 --> 00:20:55,248
Hearing of the negotiations, Steve Jobs picked up the phone.

276
00:20:55,248 --> 00:20:56,408
He pitched Amelio on NeXT.

277
00:20:56,408 --> 00:20:57,312
In December 1996, Apple announced it was acquiring NeXT for four hundred and twenty-nine million dollars in cash and stock.

278
00:20:57,312 --> 00:21:02,736
Eleven years after being thrown out of the company he founded, Steve Jobs walked back through the front doors of One Infinite Loop as an "informal advisor."

279
00:21:02,736 --> 00:21:07,584
It was the greatest Trojan horse in corporate history.

280
00:21:07,584 --> 00:22:49,464
Jobs did not stay an informal advisor for long.

281
00:22:49,464 --> 00:22:53,328
Within seven months, he orchestrated a boardroom rebellion.

282
00:22:53,328 --> 00:22:59,588
Gil Amelio was ousted.

283
00:22:59,588 --> 00:22:59,988
The board was overhauled.

284
00:22:59,988 --> 00:23:01,368
Jobs became interim CEO—the "iCEO"—

285
00:23:01,368 --> 00:23:03,072
famously taking an annual salary of exactly one dollar.

286
00:23:03,072 --> 00:23:11,040
In August 1997, Jobs walked onto the stage at Macworld Boston.

287
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:15,504
Apple was weeks away from running out of cash.

288
00:23:15,504 --> 00:23:16,804
He looked out at five thousand terrified, loyal Mac users and dropped an absolute bomb.

289
00:23:16,804 --> 00:23:18,764
A giant satellite video screen descended from the ceiling,

290
00:23:18,764 --> 00:23:19,564
and the colossal, smiling face of Bill Gates appeared live from Redmond, Washington.

291
00:23:19,564 --> 00:23:20,464
Microsoft was investing one hundred and fifty million dollars into Apple

292
00:23:20,464 --> 00:23:21,404
and guaranteeing that Microsoft Office would remain on the Mac for five years.

293
00:23:21,404 --> 00:23:22,440
The auditorium erupted into furious boos and jeers.

294
00:23:22,440 --> 00:23:27,744
Jobs stopped them with a line of cold, clinical pragmatism:

295
00:23:27,744 --> 00:23:31,744
"We have to let go of this notion that for Apple to win, Microsoft has to lose.

296
00:23:31,744 --> 00:23:36,552
If we want Apple to be healthy again, we have to look at where Apple can do a really good job."

297
00:23:36,552 --> 00:23:39,272
Next, Jobs marched into Apple’s product planning room.

298
00:23:39,272 --> 00:23:39,672
The whiteboard was covered in dozens of confusing models.

299
00:23:39,672 --> 00:23:40,608
Jobs grabbed a black marker, wiped the board clean,

300
00:23:40,608 --> 00:23:41,628
and drew a simple two-by-two grid.

301
00:23:41,628 --> 00:23:42,668
Across the top, he wrote "Consumer" and "Pro."

302
00:23:42,668 --> 00:23:43,632
Down the side, he wrote "Desktop" and "Portable."

303
00:23:43,632 --> 00:23:45,432
"Here is what we are going to build," Jobs declared.

304
00:23:45,432 --> 00:23:49,752
"Four great computers.

305
00:23:49,752 --> 00:23:51,096
Everything else, we cancel."

306
00:23:51,096 --> 00:23:52,716
He eliminated seventy percent of Apple’s product line overnight.

307
00:23:52,716 --> 00:23:53,716
Three thousand employees were laid off.

308
00:23:53,716 --> 00:23:55,336
He cancelled the Newton handheld PDA.

309
00:23:55,336 --> 00:23:56,456
He shuttered outside software clones.

310
00:23:56,456 --> 00:23:57,360
The bloat was surgically amputated.

311
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:59,160
Then, Jobs walked down to Apple’s design laboratory

312
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:00,800
and discovered a young, soft-spoken British designer named Jony Ive.

313
00:24:00,800 --> 00:24:03,624
Ive had his resignation letter in his pocket, exhausted by corporate executives who cared only about cost-cutting.

314
00:24:03,624 --> 00:24:06,844
Jobs looked at Ive’s radical prototypes

315
00:24:06,844 --> 00:24:09,432
and recognized a soulmate who shared his fanatical devotion to the purity of physical materials.

316
00:24:09,432 --> 00:24:15,720
In May 1998, they unveiled the machine that saved Apple: the iMac G3.

317
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:16,480
It was a teardrop-shaped computer made of translucent Bondi blue plastic, glowing with friendly warmth.

318
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:18,160
It threw away the floppy drive,

319
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:21,340
championed USB,

320
00:24:21,340 --> 00:24:21,740
and featured a handle on the top.

321
00:24:21,740 --> 00:24:22,140
Critics mocked the handle, pointing out that a desktop computer is rarely carried around.

322
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:22,540
Jobs replied: "If there's a handle, it makes a relationship possible.

323
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:22,940
It invites you to touch it."

324
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:27,984
The iMac became the fastest-selling computer in Apple’s history.

325
00:24:27,984 --> 00:24:37,656
In 1998, Apple turned an eighty-seven-million-dollar profit.

326
00:24:37,656 --> 00:24:39,096
The bleeding was officially stopped.

327
00:24:39,096 --> 00:24:42,096
Next, Jobs realized that the personal computer was no longer just a calculation tool;

328
00:24:42,096 --> 00:24:42,496
it was becoming the "digital hub" for personal creative lives—camcorders, cameras, and music players.

329
00:24:42,496 --> 00:24:42,896
In early 2001, music was in total chaos.

330
00:24:42,896 --> 00:24:44,544
Napster had shattered the traditional record industry.

331
00:24:44,544 --> 00:24:47,224
Millions of people were downloading pirated MP3s onto clunky, battery-draining portable players that held barely twenty songs.

332
00:24:47,224 --> 00:24:49,524
Jobs saw an opening.

333
00:24:49,524 --> 00:25:03,264
Apple developed an ultra-compact player built around a tiny five-gigabyte Toshiba hard drive.

334
00:25:03,264 --> 00:25:08,520
During development, an engineer told Jobs the prototype couldn't possibly be made any smaller.

335
00:25:08,520 --> 00:25:12,768
Jobs walked over to an office aquarium, dropped the prototype into the water,

336
00:25:12,768 --> 00:25:17,008
and pointed at the bubbles rising to the surface.

337
00:25:17,008 --> 00:25:17,408
"Those are air bubbles," Jobs said coldly.

338
00:25:17,408 --> 00:25:20,228
"That means there is empty space inside.

339
00:25:20,228 --> 00:25:20,628
Make it smaller."

340
00:25:20,628 --> 00:25:21,072
On October 23, 2001, six weeks after the September 11 attacks,

341
00:25:21,072 --> 00:25:23,760
Jobs pulled a device the size of a deck of playing cards from his pocket: the iPod.

342
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:25,680
His pitch was an absolute masterpiece of copywriting: "One thousand songs in your pocket."

343
00:25:25,680 --> 00:25:26,480
Two years later, Jobs cornered the terrified major record labels, who were watching physical CD sales collapse,

344
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:27,240
and convinced them to sell individual digital tracks on the iTunes Music Store for ninety-nine cents each.

345
00:25:27,240 --> 00:25:28,740
Over four hundred million iPods were sold.

346
00:25:28,740 --> 00:25:36,456
The stark white headphone cords dangling from people's ears became the ultimate cultural status symbol of the twenty-first century.

347
00:25:36,456 --> 00:25:38,316
Apple was no longer just a computer company.

348
00:25:38,316 --> 00:25:41,040
It was an undisputed titan of global media.

349
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:43,220
Yet by 2005, Steve Jobs recognized a mortal threat to his empire.

350
00:25:43,220 --> 00:25:56,256
Mobile cellphones were becoming smarter.

351
00:25:56,256 --> 00:26:03,504
Jobs realized that the moment mobile phones learned to play music reliably, the iPod would be wiped out.

352
00:26:03,504 --> 00:26:07,488
"If we don't cannibalize ourselves," Jobs told his executive team, "someone else will."

353
00:26:07,488 --> 00:26:14,376
For two years, Apple lived under top-secret military-grade lockdown on Project Purple.

354
00:26:14,376 --> 00:26:15,536
Engineers worked around the clock in windowless rooms with blacked-out glass, signing non-disclosure agreements that forbade them from telling their own spouses what they were building.

355
00:26:15,536 --> 00:26:17,356
They abandoned mechanical keyboards entirely and developed capacitive multi-touch:

356
00:26:17,356 --> 00:26:18,856
a display that allowed human fingers to directly flick, scroll, pinch, and zoom on a sheet of glass.

357
00:26:18,856 --> 00:26:19,976
Jobs made a secret deal with AT&T.

358
00:26:19,976 --> 00:26:20,516
In exchange for exclusive carrier rights, Jobs demanded something no phone manufacturer had ever secured in telecom history:

359
00:26:20,516 --> 00:26:21,396
complete, absolute control over the device's hardware, software, and industrial design, with zero carrier bloatware,

360
00:26:21,396 --> 00:26:22,632
plus a monthly cut of every customer's data bill.

361
00:26:22,632 --> 00:26:32,040
On January 9, 2007, Steve Jobs took the stage at Macworld San Francisco.

362
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:37,944
"Every once in a while, a revolutionary product comes along that changes everything," he began.

363
00:26:37,944 --> 00:26:42,408
"Today, we are introducing three revolutionary products.

364
00:26:42,408 --> 00:26:46,328
The first is a widescreen iPod with touch controls.

365
00:26:46,328 --> 00:26:48,696
The second is a revolutionary mobile phone.

366
00:26:48,696 --> 00:26:49,496
And the third is a breakthrough internet communications device."

367
00:26:49,496 --> 00:26:50,176
He repeated the three names as the audience cheered.

368
00:26:50,176 --> 00:26:52,104
Then came the punchline: "An iPod.

369
00:26:52,104 --> 00:26:54,924
A phone.

370
00:26:54,924 --> 00:26:56,664
An internet communicator.

371
00:26:56,664 --> 00:27:00,240
Are you getting it?

372
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:03,936
These are not three separate devices.

373
00:27:03,936 --> 00:27:41,208
This is one device.

374
00:27:41,208 --> 00:27:49,512
And we are calling it: iPhone."

375
00:27:49,512 --> 00:28:55,872
The demo was a technological miracle.

376
00:28:55,872 --> 00:28:56,852
Behind the stage curtain, software engineers were taking shots of scotch whiskey to calm their nerves.

377
00:28:56,852 --> 00:28:57,792
The phone barely worked; its memory was so unstable that if Jobs tapped the apps in the wrong sequence, the phone would crash completely.

378
00:28:57,792 --> 00:28:58,812
Jobs navigated the exact sequence flawlessly.

379
00:28:58,812 --> 00:29:00,152
When he walked off that stage, the legacy mobile giants were dead men walking.

380
00:29:00,152 --> 00:29:55,440
The CEO of BlackBerry publicly dismissed it, insisting enterprise users would never surrender physical keyboards.

381
00:29:55,440 --> 00:30:01,776
Steve Ballmer, CEO of Microsoft, laughed on camera: "Five hundred dollars fully subsidized?

382
00:30:01,776 --> 00:30:02,796
It is the most expensive phone in the world, and it doesn't appeal to business customers!"

383
00:30:02,796 --> 00:30:03,616
Within five years, BlackBerry was completely wiped out.

384
00:30:03,616 --> 00:30:07,416
Nokia’s multi-billion-dollar mobile business collapsed into irrelevance.

385
00:30:07,416 --> 00:30:07,816
In 2008, Apple launched the App Store with just five hundred apps.

386
00:30:07,816 --> 00:30:08,216
It spawned the modern smartphone economy: Uber, Instagram, WhatsApp, DoorDash, and TikTok.

387
00:30:08,216 --> 00:30:24,840
Apple extracted a thirty percent commission on every digital purchase—creating the most lucrative toll booth in corporate history.

388
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:28,248
Behind the unprecedented triumph, Steve Jobs was quietly running out of time.

389
00:30:28,248 --> 00:30:29,748
He had been diagnosed with a rare neuroendocrine tumor in 2003.

390
00:30:29,748 --> 00:30:31,512
For eight years, he fought the disease while orchestrating the most prolific product run in business history:

391
00:30:31,512 --> 00:30:33,552
the MacBook Air,

392
00:30:33,552 --> 00:30:36,272
the iPad,

393
00:30:36,272 --> 00:30:36,672
and Apple’s custom A-series silicon chips.

394
00:30:36,672 --> 00:30:37,092
In August 2011, knowing his body was failing, Jobs resigned as CEO

395
00:30:37,092 --> 00:30:38,952
and handed the reins to his Chief Operating Officer: Tim Cook.

396
00:30:38,952 --> 00:30:39,352
Cook was an Alabama-born industrial engineer.

397
00:30:39,352 --> 00:30:40,968
Calm, soft-spoken, and disciplined,

398
00:30:40,968 --> 00:30:45,728
he was the logistical genius who had closed Apple's factories, outsourced manufacturing to Asia,

399
00:30:45,728 --> 00:30:47,184
and reduced Apple's inventory holding time to forty-eight hours.

400
00:30:47,184 --> 00:30:50,364
Jobs gave Cook one final, sacred piece of advice:

401
00:30:50,364 --> 00:30:52,872
"Never ask what Steve would do. Just do what’s right."

402
00:30:52,872 --> 00:30:59,760
On October 5, 2011, Steve Jobs died at his home in Palo Alto at age fifty-six.

403
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:04,224
Across the world, Apple stores became spontaneous memorials.

404
00:31:04,224 --> 00:31:08,688
People placed candles, flowers, and real apples with bite marks taken out of them on sidewalks from Tokyo to New York.

405
00:31:08,688 --> 00:31:17,808
Wall Street predicted that without Jobs’s charismatic lightning, Apple would slowly wither into a legacy hardware maker.

406
00:31:17,808 --> 00:31:24,792
Instead, Tim Cook turned Apple into the most terrifyingly efficient profit machine in human history.

407
00:31:24,792 --> 00:31:29,520
He launched the Apple Watch, outselling the entire Swiss watchmaking industry combined.

408
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:35,304
He launched AirPods, which generated more revenue than most Fortune 500 corporations.

409
00:31:35,304 --> 00:31:42,288
He transitioned the Mac away from Intel chips to Apple Silicon, delivering unprecedented battery life and speed.

410
00:31:42,288 --> 00:31:49,992
In August 2018, Apple became the first public American corporation to cross a one-trillion-dollar valuation.

411
00:31:49,992 --> 00:31:51,952
Two years later, it crossed two trillion.

412
00:31:51,952 --> 00:32:01,872
In 2023, it crossed three trillion.

413
00:32:01,872 --> 00:32:05,928
Yet as the numbers entered the stratosphere, the moral cost of the empire became impossible to ignore.

414
00:32:05,928 --> 00:32:10,768
In Zhengzhou, China—a sprawling manufacturing metropolis known as "iPhone City"—

415
00:32:10,768 --> 00:32:16,872
over two hundred thousand workers labor in twelve-hour shifts on assembly lines, piecing together camera sensors and batteries.

416
00:32:16,872 --> 00:32:22,320
When pressure mounted, the factories had to erect anti-suicide netting along dormitory roofs to catch falling workers.

417
00:32:22,320 --> 00:32:25,560
Governments around the world launched massive antitrust investigations, accusing Apple of running an abusive monopoly with its thirty percent App Store tax.

418
00:32:25,560 --> 00:32:28,584
Developers sued.

419
00:32:28,584 --> 00:32:43,536
The European Union forced Apple to abandon its proprietary Lightning connector in favor of universal USB-C.

420
00:32:43,536 --> 00:32:45,896
And millions of human beings began to realize that the glowing glass rectangles had subtly reshaped human consciousness.

421
00:32:45,896 --> 00:32:47,636
Families sitting at dinner tables in total silence, scrolling through algorithmically tailored feeds.

422
00:32:47,636 --> 00:32:49,680
Teenagers trapped in dopamine loops of notifications, likes, and red badge alerts.

423
00:32:49,680 --> 00:32:58,656
The company that in 1984 promised to smash Big Brother had built the most seductive, inescapable surveillance ecosystem on earth.

424
00:32:58,656 --> 00:32:59,216
It was not forced upon humanity by a dictator.

425
00:32:59,216 --> 00:33:00,396
Humanity bought it willingly.

426
00:33:00,396 --> 00:33:01,996
Because it was comfortable.

427
00:33:01,996 --> 00:33:03,144
Because it was beautiful.

428
00:33:03,144 --> 00:33:06,264
Because it worked.

429
00:33:06,264 --> 00:33:08,684
In 1976, two broke dreamers in an unpainted Los Altos garage started soldering circuits together

430
00:33:08,684 --> 00:33:12,312
because they believed that technology should belong to the individual, not the corporation.

431
00:33:12,312 --> 00:34:56,352
Fifty years later, their creation governs the private communications, memories, and financial transactions of over a billion human beings.

432
00:34:56,352 --> 00:34:57,612
Steve Jobs once called the computer a bicycle for our minds—

433
00:34:57,612 --> 00:34:58,632
a tool that would amplify human capability beyond our natural limits.

434
00:34:58,632 --> 00:34:59,572
Looking at the world today, the only question that remains is who is riding whom.

435
00:34:59,572 --> 00:35:16,872
From an oil-stained garage on Crist Drive to a three-trillion-dollar glass empire that conquered the world.

436
00:35:16,872 --> 00:35:24,572
If you felt the weight of this story, hit like and subscribe for the complete history of more empires, one founder at a time.

437
00:35:24,572 --> 00:35:30,692
And tell us in the comments below: what was the very first Apple product you ever held in your hands?

438
00:35:30,692 --> 00:35:33,892
The II, the iPod, or the iPhone?

439
00:35:33,892 --> 00:35:35,088
See you at the next build.
